// lug: scaffolded-spoke-ships-schedule-circles-with-no-wheel-clock
// A spoke scaffolded from the current cut either gets the clock its own
// scheduled circles need, or does not receive circles it cannot run --
// and both halves are read from canon, never hardcoded in the scaffolder:
//   1. a REAL fresh scaffold (the same bootstrap path `wcl init` runs) is
//      born with canon/spoke.wheel-clock.yaml, byte-for-byte the canon
//      default reference/wheel-clock/spoke.wheel-clock.yaml, and
//      circle-audit on that real scaffold reports 0 PHANTOM caused by a
//      missing wheel clock -- the lug's first acceptance, on a scaffold,
//      not a fixture stand-in;
//   2. the two collateral OPEN circles the lug names (design-salvage-review,
//      scheduler-failure-operator-channel) close on that scaffold, with
//      neither reference file edited;
//   3. a circle marked as requiring a job the target clock does not declare
//      is EXCLUDED from the scaffold; one requiring a job it does declare
//      is kept -- decided per circle from its own requires_wheel_clock_job;
//   4. the cadence is read from canon: change the number in the scaffold's
//      own clock file and the real scheduler fires the job on the new
//      cadence, proven by ticking it;
//   5. a cut built FOR an instance is judged against that instance's clock
//      (options.clockRoot), so a spoke whose clock declares every job --
//      wheel-hub's shape -- receives every schedule circle, unchanged;
//   6. this framework's own real root (read-only) audits with 0 schedule
//      PHANTOMs: the 14 that printed at every session start are either
